---
// The experience argument, which used to be only implied. Every competitor in
// this space sells a framework with a trademark on it — maturity models,
// governance baselines, accreditation cycles — and none of them has run the
// function they are advising on. That is the one claim a competitor cannot
// copy by rewriting their homepage next week, so it gets made outright rather
// than left to be inferred from two bios.
//
// "Forty years" is the defensible number and is deliberately understated:
// Elliott's twenty plus Mac from 2005 lands nearer forty-one. Do not round it
// up. Any buyer checks that on LinkedIn in ninety seconds, and the credibility
// argument is the thing that breaks.
//
// The discipline list stays inside the sentence. Seven competencies from two
// people, set out as a bulleted panel, reads generalist; inside a sentence,
// following the claim that we ran them, it reads as range.
const argument = [
  'Forty years between us of running the jobs we now help people fix. Operations, sales, marketing, leadership, finance, logistics, and the data underneath all of it. Not studied. Run, with the P&L attached.',
  'Experience is the hardest thing left to buy. Most of what gets sold as AI consulting comes from people whose entire operating history is the deck they are showing you. When we walk into a plant or a yard or a back office, we are not learning the business for the first time. We have been the person on the other side of the desk, and usually with the same problem.',
  'There is no bench, no account manager, and nobody junior learning on your engagement. The two people who diagnose your operation are the two who build what comes next.',
];

// Both bios in third person, structurally parallel. Previously Elliott's ran
// in first person before his name while Mac's ran in third — one voice now.
const partners = [
  {
    name: 'Elliott English',
    role: 'Co-Founder · Operations & Build',
    body: [
      'Elliott spent twenty years running operations inside organizations far larger than the ones he serves now — the Pentagon, Morgan Stanley, healthcare, pharma, and a telehealth division he built from nothing. Derivatives metrics and fraud operations at one end, a Pentagon desk in Qatar at the other.',
      'The thread through all of it was the same: find where a complex organization is genuinely broken and fix the system rather than the symptom. He does that work at owner-operator scale now for one reason — the blockages are visible, the fixes pay back inside a quarter, and the person who can approve the change is in the room. He has run the reports you are running by hand, at a scale where doing it by hand was not an option.',
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Mac Steele',
    role: 'Co-Founder · Business Development',
    body: [
      'Mac has spent his career carrying proven technology into industries that had never used it — proxy tabulation for institutional investors, RFID inventory control, and fifteen years moving established manufacturing technology from brick into metals, mining, and steel.',
      // The memo's line ended "carrying the number at the bottom of the page
      // personally." That phrase already does work twice on this page — the
      // roster section has "carries the P&L personally" and the fit list has
      // "the number at the bottom of it is personally yours" — so the tail is
      // reworded here. The ownership claim itself is confirmed.
      'The job never changed: find a company\'s real problem, match it to technology that actually solves it, and stay long enough to help them pull the value out. Twenty years of it as an owner and an operator, carrying the losses himself when he got it wrong. He finds the businesses with the right problems. Elliott builds the fix.',
    ],
  },
];

// Board experience, placed after the bios. The least copyable thing on the
// site, and it matters because of who we actually sell to: nearly the whole
// book is family-owned or owner-operated — a family firm with a board of
// managing directors, a Sheffield family manufacturer, and a run of
// owner-operated businesses behind them.
//
// BOUNDARY TO HOLD: this says we understand the dynamics. It does not say we
// sell succession planning or governance consulting. Do not let it drift into
// services we do not deliver, or the first person who asks for a family
// charter puts us in an awkward spot.
//
// The named chairmanship carries more weight than the list of seats — keep it
// specific. The closing line is the one that gets a reaction: it gives the
// owner permission to raise the thing he was not going to raise, which is
// usually the real bottleneck.
//
// "Mac on the board, Elliott in front of it" is load-bearing and accurate:
// Mac has held director and chairman seats, Elliott's board exposure is
// nonprofit service and presenting to investment subcommittees. An unqualified
// "the room we have spent our careers in" would have claimed director seats
// for both, and a buyer checks that on LinkedIn in ninety seconds.
// Cross-border operations. Deliberately NOT an eighth service line — the page
// already carries seven disciplines, three delivery models, three modules,
// hardware, legacy systems and board experience, and one more offer would make
// us look like a shop that does everything. It goes on as proof instead.
//
// It earns the space because cross-border operations create exactly the
// problem we already solve: subsidiaries on different systems, different
// currencies, different reporting calendars, and nobody at the top able to see
// one consolidated picture.
//
// BOUNDARY TO HOLD: this is not a market-entry advisory offer. No
// route-to-market studies, no distributor searches, no export compliance. The
// block claims operational experience across borders and stops there.
//
// The closing line is the argument — anyone can claim international
// experience; having sat on both ends of the same reporting request is
// specific and true, and it is what a European MD looking at the US wants to
// hear. The German is a hard credential to fake, which is why it stays in.
const borders = [
  'Mac spent twenty years running commercial operations across the US, Europe, Australia and South America, pulling subsidiaries onto one framework so a business that size could see itself as one company. Saudi Arabia, Australia, Germany, Latin America. Fluent German, working Spanish, and a fairly clear-eyed view of what actually goes wrong when a company crosses an ocean.',
  'If you are a European manufacturer looking at North America, or a North American company trying to build a position in Europe, the operational problems are the ones we already work on. Systems that were never designed to consolidate. Pricing that drifts between regions because nobody can compare it directly. A subsidiary that reports on a different calendar in a different currency and takes six weeks to become a number anyone trusts. Local teams solving the same problem three different ways because nobody has ever seen all three.',
];

const board = [
  'Mac has served on boards for most of his career, including as chairman of an international manufacturer with operations on four continents, alongside director seats across manufacturing and construction materials. Most of our clients are family-owned or owner-operated, and that is not a coincidence.',
  'The hardest problems in a family business are usually not operational. They are about who decides, what the next generation is owed, what gets said at the table and what gets said in the parking lot afterward. A recommendation that ignores all of that is a recommendation that does not get implemented.',
  'We know what a board needs to see before it will approve something. We know why a good idea sits for two years. And we know how to write a proposal that survives a room full of directors who have never met us, because that is the room we have spent our careers in — Mac on the board, Elliott in front of it.',
];
---

<section id="about" class="about section surface-ivory">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <div class="section-head section-head-center reveal">
      <p class="section-label">Who you're working with</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Two operators, not an agency.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="about-argument reveal" style="--reveal-delay: 60ms">
      {argument.map(para => <p class="argument-para">{para}</p>)}
    </div>

    <div class="partner-grid">
      {partners.map((p, i) => (
        <article class="partner-card reveal" style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 90}ms`}>
          <header class="partner-head">
            <h3 class="partner-name">{p.name}</h3>
            <p class="partner-role">{p.role}</p>
          </header>
          {p.body.map(para => <p class="partner-para">{para}</p>)}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="beyond reveal" aria-labelledby="beyond-heading">
      <p class="beyond-label" id="beyond-heading">What we bring beyond the build</p>

      <div class="beyond-grid">
      <article class="beyond-part">
        <h3 class="beyond-title">We have sat on the other side of the table.</h3>
        {board.map(para => <p class="beyond-para">{para}</p>)}
        <p class="beyond-close">
          You can talk to us about the business and about the family, and
          neither conversation will surprise us.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="beyond-part">
        <h3 class="beyond-title">Across borders</h3>
        {borders.map(para => <p class="beyond-para">{para}</p>)}
        <p class="beyond-close">
          We have been the parent company trying to get one honest picture, and
          we have been the subsidiary being asked for it. Both jobs are harder
          than they look from the other side.
        </p>
      </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Centred column, left-aligned text. Three centred paragraphs at lead size
     is more centred prose than the eye will carry. */
  .about-argument {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 56px);
    transition-delay: var(--reveal-delay, 0ms);
  }

  .argument-para {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--charcoal);
  }

  .argument-para:first-child {
    font-size: var(--step-lead);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .argument-para + .argument-para {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .argument-para:last-child {
    color: var(--warm-gray);
  }

  .partner-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(20px, 2.6vw, 28px);
  }

  .partner-card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: clamp(28px, 3.2vw, 40px);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    transition-delay: var(--reveal-delay, 0ms);
  }

  .partner-head {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .partner-name {
    font-size: var(--step-h3);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .partner-role {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--terracotta);
  }

  .partner-para {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--charcoal);
  }

  .partner-para + .partner-para {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Board experience and cross-border operations share one frame rather than
     stacking as two more panels. Both are claims about the pair rather than
     either partner, both need the bios read first to land, and neither is a
     service — the shared label is what stops them reading as offers. */
  .beyond {
    margin: clamp(28px, 3.5vw, 40px) auto 0;
    padding: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 40px);
    background: var(--ivory-dark);
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .beyond-label {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--step-eyebrow);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--terracotta);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* Two columns, echoing the bio grid directly above. Stacked, these two run
     past 1200px of panel and leave half the width empty. */
  .beyond-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 44px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .beyond-part + .beyond-part {
    padding-left: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 44px);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .beyond-title {
    font-size: var(--step-h3);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .beyond-para {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--warm-gray);
  }

  .beyond-para + .beyond-para {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Each part closes on its argument — the line that gives the owner
     permission to raise what he was not going to raise, and the line that
     makes the international claim specific rather than a boast. */
  .beyond-close {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--charcoal);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--terracotta);
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .partner-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }

    .beyond-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }

    /* Stacked, the divider has to run horizontally instead. */
    .beyond-part + .beyond-part {
      padding-left: 0;
      border-left: none;
      padding-top: var(--space-6);
      border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    }
  }
</style>
